# std.par: running a function on each element of a List, an array, or a
# slice, on several tasks at once, each taking chunks of the elements in
# turn, or each holding a state of its own. The checker types the
# calls, as it does `task.all`.
#
# - `par.map(?xs, f, limit: n)` gives a List of `f(x)` for each `x`, in
#   the order of `xs`.
# - `par.each(!xs, f, limit: n)` calls `f(!x)` for each element, which
#   it may write.
# - `par.workers(!states, ?items, f)` runs a task for each state, which
#   calls `f(!s, x)` with its own state on the next item it takes, in
#   the order of `items`, until none is left. It gives a List of the
#   values in the order of the items, or nothing when `f` gives nothing. Each
#   state goes to one task, to write, so it must be sendable.
#
# `f` is a closure written in the call, or a function's name. Every task
# reads what the closure captures, so each capture must sync, and none
# is a write view. When `f` can fail, so can the call: the first failure
# cancels the other tasks, and the call fails with it. `limit`, of `map`
# and `each`, is how many tasks run at once, by default one per worker; with one, or few
# elements, the calls run in turn on the calling task.
